course: dictionary
lesson: 12248
title: Formal words: constitute
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Constitute is a formal word, the kind you find in rules, reports and books. It has a few meanings, but they all share one idea: the parts that make up a whole.
explain: «Ten players constitute a full football team.» The ten players are the parts, and together they make up the team. Swap in make up: «Ten players make up a full football team» still makes sense, so constitute means make up.
explain: «Breaking this rule constitutes cheating.» Here constitute means counts as. Breaking the rule is counted as cheating. Try your meaning in the sentence, and if it still makes sense, you have the right one.
explain: Constitute can also mean set up an official group, as in «The class constituted a new reading group.» That is a little rarer, so look at the words around it to be sure.
fact: Constitute is a verb, so it changes: constitutes, constituted, constituting.
fact: «Constitute» means to make up or form a whole, or to be or count as something.
fact: «Constitute a danger» means to be a danger.
fact: Formal words like constitute are often found in rules, reports and exams, so it is worth learning what they mean.
exercise: «Twelve months constitute one year.» What does constitute mean here? | Shout at | *Make up | Jump over | Forget
exercise: «Running across the road constitutes a danger to you.» What does this mean? | Is a fun game | *Counts as a danger | Stops the danger | Makes the road wider
exercise: «The class constituted a new reading group last week.» What does constituted mean? | Lost | Forgot | Ate | *Set up
test: «Five players constitute a team on the court.» What does constitute mean here? | Lose | Run away from | Cheer for | *Make up
test: «Last year, the new rules constituted a big change.» What does constituted mean here? | Were lost | *Were counted as a big change | Were printed in colour | Were broken
exam: In «Girls constitute half of the class», what does constitute mean? | Hide | *Make up | Stop | Shout
